welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Robert Shiller the Arthur M oaken  professor of economics as well as a  professor of Finance at Yale School of  Management the new york times  best-selling economist is probably the  only person to have predicted both the  stock market bubble of 2000 and the real  estate bubble that led up to the  subprime mortgage meltdown yet he has no  apologist for the sins of finance today  we talk with Professor Shiller about his  new and very timely book finance and the  good society where he argues that rather  than condemning finance we need to  reclaim it for the common good  welcome professor Shiller pleasure to be  here let's begin with why you wrote this  book well I've been teaching finance for  25 years at Yale and I am launching  young people on their careers  now Yale has an open Yale version of my  lectures that are broadcast all over the  world so I'm developing people to be  either directly in finance or to be  somehow involved in in finance I think  ours I thought of myself as preparing  people to live in our modern society  which is I call financial capitalism but  the the crisis the financial crisis that  we've had since 2007 has a lot of people  doubting the value of finance and  there's a lot of angry criticism Occupy  Wall Street sure and so I feel that sort  of a quandary that I'm preparing the  students for what I'm sure is a good  thing on the whole but people don't  appreciate that and I wanted to write a  book which not only shows the value of  finance but shows the way our society is  going to evolve as a  nancial capitalistic society okay  in your book you write financial  capitalism is an invention and the  process of inventing it is hardly over I  don't think most people think of it in  terms of that and do you think that's  part of the problem  definitely it's part of the problem we  appreciate when Apple computer invents  some new system we are kind of awed by  that and we think that they deserve the  money they earn right but when when we  do something like securitized mortgages  in finance it looks a little bit like  somebody's scheme to get rich and the  benefits to society from securitizing  mortgages are harder to see it's not  like you get some new fancy electronic  gizmo that flashes and does amazing  things it's just that you can get a  mortgage at a lower rate and you  actually get the mortgage to buy a house  and so it's not as visible but actually  designing something like a securitized  mortgage takes a lot of work and  creative inventiveness in terms of the  reputation of Wall Street today being so  porin and so many people have lost money  yet some people still continue to make  millions upon millions of dollars let's  look at some of the ways finance  historically has contributed to society  well I would say that we are living in a  period where the examples of the  advantages of finance are all around us  all over the world we are seeing the  phenomenal growth of China of India and  in fact of just about any major segment  of any emerging economy and what's  happening is that the financialization  of the economies of the world and what  it's doing is it's bringing people into  modernity it's bringing them good  healthcare clean homes all sorts of  wonderful things  and I really think those things would  not have been possible if we stayed in a  traditional old-fashioned the economy  okay what do you think the role of  Finance should be for the good of  society well I think it it should be  more disbursed it should be broader than  it is now  more democratic for a Democrat that's  the term I used in the book that there  has been a process over centuries  towards the democratization of Finance  by that I mean if you go back to the  18th century not many people own stocks  there weren't very many corporations but  that has read out and developed into  much more now that is true but for  instance take the situation we have on  our hands now where many people invested  in the stock market and then lost  significant portions of their portfolio  how can we prevent or somehow negate  that from happening down the road well  the world or can we write the world is  fundamentally uncertain we don't know  the future and financial market  fluctuations reflect that but the the  uncertainty of the world can be managed  substantially we have a theory of risk  management there's a mathematical theory  of it now and the there is also a  behavioral theory that explains people's  psychological problems in managing risk  but even if we don't know the future  there are things we can do to lessen the  impact of them like what well like  diversify invest and diversify  when you say diversify what do you mean  that means the old adage is don't put  all your eggs in one basket that makes  it sound obvious but in that as a matter  of fact most people don't even today  so we we have to get the word that we  need better investor education we need  better advisors we need advisors that  are available for the more general  public and I think that we're moving in  the right direction you know it's part  of the progress of civilization so I  think that as the years go by in coming  decades we will see financial  institutions even more an integral part  of our lives and they will look  different the next 10 20 30 years will  be different how do you do you have any  insights as to what differences there  may be well it has to do with electronic  technology and we are all very used now  to search engines and algorithms of one  sort or another I think that we will  have retail products that will help  people manage their risks right now not  many people avail themselves of  financial technology they they go  through life owning a house that might  lose value and suddenly they're wiped  out oh they might specialize in a career  and then the career turns out not to  have been a wise choice  like you could become a nuclear engineer  and we have we have bad events and then  suddenly you're not you're in trouble  these are all risk that people face and  as we democratize finance I think what I  think we will see more and more help in  managing these risk well that's good  because I think people do need help I  think for some people it can be  overwhelming to try and understand what  is going on in the financial market and  how best you can navigate it to you know  reap the benefits for yourself and a lot  of people put their trust and financial  advisers and that perhaps may not always  be that the best way to go and and in  terms of that I mean you know you talk  about the sleaze factor in your book how  can we move away from that well the  financial industry is off  opportunities to make money in sleazy  manipulative way right a little bit like  politics you know they don't make maybe  money but they they have a sleaze factor  problem as well so I think that we need  how do we deal with it you know in my  book I describe it as a mall issue right  for my students or other any people who  are in the industry and it it poses  difficult problems one problem is that  there's always going to be sleazy people  around you and how do you deal with that  you get dragged down by them and your  own reputation is harmed by these other  people but I don't want ones theme is  that I don't I think we have to be  self-assured those of us who are doing  business or somehow related to finance  self assured that I am a good person I  have a moral standard and this is what  we teach in business schools of a kind  of moral standard that underpins I have  a sense of optimism in the book that  most people are not sleazy and you have  to be careful not to be over over  reacting to some elements of sleazy you  know that everyone messes up there's  always some people I think you know the  Occupy Wall Street people are very  focused on some sleazy examples but it's  not it's not that bad a situation and I  think it will gradually get better and  regulation helps with that and and we're  improving our regulatory apparatus  especially with the crash of the market  and and I think I don't many things have  been put in place to a guard against  that happening down the road some  conclusions in your book how can we  reclaim finance and harness its power  for the good of society give us some  examples well I can give you historical  examples of important invention the  stock market is an invention it's a very  smart invention in terms of what it  provides for  risk-management what it provides for  human psychology getting people involved  getting them incentivized to do  coordinated work to allocate I could go  on and on it sound like it but it's  something that has to be improved it's  still not done and there are and when  you say it's it's not done yet it needs  to be improved what can you cite one or  two things that can be done to improve  it well one example that is only about  two years old it's not my idea but I  talked about it in the book is and it's  a little subtle a little bit hard to  capsulize there's a new kind of  corporation called the benefit  corporation okay which has now created  in eight US states it's a u.s. thing I  think it will be exported to other  countries eventually but right now it's  very small it's just starting a benefit  corporation is different from a standard  corporation in that its corporate  charter says that it's not exclusively  in the business of making profits it has  two objectives a profits for the  shareholders be some other objective  that is stated in the at the time of the  founding of the company in its Charter  and so you've got dual objectives and  these other objectives are may be in  conflict with the profit-making  motive but I I suspect that this new  corporate form will be important and  that even people who are interested in  investing for the profits the company  makes will choose to invest in that kind  of company and in fact that kind of  company may turn out it's an experiment  now it may turn out to be better at  making profits than a conventional  corporation because it's more humane it  could be if the if some of the other  purposes are human purposes about the  community or society or the environment  and I think that this is an important  innovation which we  are just starting now here and in the  21st century well that's exciting I  hadn't heard about that is it in terms  of an entity is it legally different  from a regular corporation okay the law  in the United States has imposed a duty  of loyalty to the board of directors of  a company loyalty to the shareholders  that is cardinal in US law and in other  countries as well and so a director who  does something for the community would  be violating the duty of loyalty and  could be subject to a lawsuit for doing  that so it's definitely a different  legal structure that we now have and I  think more and more states are going to  adopt it and foreign countries will  adopt it are these being traded now  currently and whilst they are but I  don't have a big final question  professor if there was one thing that we  can take from your book what would you  want it to be well having written a long  book there are many things I know I know  but I think I think that we should be  thinking of financial capitalism as a  kind of Organization for society that  respects human differences respects the  different contributions that different  people can make and it doesn't put  people in a straightjacket it doesn't  put them in a you know you belong to  some institution now take orders from  your boss that may happen too but what  I'm saying is there's an element of  self-reliance and an element of  everyone's on the lookout for how to  make a better society we have in it will  be integrated with our modern electronic  new capabilities in search  but I think that people with this the  world is never going to be perfect but I  think that people will be able to find  their purposes they'll find fulfillment  of their deepest purposes better it's  not about making money money is only to  be spent for some purpose and I think  what I want my students and anyone who  reads this book is to come into business  or finance with a high-minded  purpose we don't tell you what the  purpose is but we you know we hope that  anyone who does go into business or  finance will have some higher purposes  that ultimately all of this driving is  is aimed at very good finance and the  good Society fascinating stuff thank you  so much for being here today and sharing  it with us my pleasure for more  information about Professor Robert  Shiller please visit our website at Yale  ddu backslash Macmillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the Macmillan report made possible  through the funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Centre for International  and area studies at Yale

